      "description": "Understand CT and MRI services in Iran, the history of scanner diffusion, and the limits of equipment counts as measures of healthcare access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-technologies",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different technologies answer different questions",
        "content": "An imaging service produces pictures that help clinicians investigate structures inside the body. Two important technologies are computed tomography, usually called CT, and magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI. Understanding their differences makes it easier to interpret discussions of equipment investment in Iran: counting machines together can obscure the different services they provide. [s1][s2]\n\nCT uses a rotating X-ray source and computer processing to construct cross-sectional images. These images can also be assembled into three-dimensional views. Its applications include investigating injuries, internal bleeding, tumors, and abnormalities in different organs. Because CT uses X-rays, radiation exposure forms part of its clinical risk–benefit assessment. A detailed image is useful when it answers an appropriate clinical question; image production alone does not establish a diagnosis. [s1]\n\nMRI uses strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses to generate images. It is particularly useful for examining soft tissues, including the brain, spinal cord, muscles, and ligaments. It does not use the ionizing X-rays employed by CT. This distinction does not make every MRI examination suitable for every person: magnetic fields, implanted devices, and other safety considerations require appropriate screening. These are general technological principles, rather than evidence about the performance of any particular Iranian center. [s2]"
      ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-diffusion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How advanced imaging spread",
        "content": "An early study of MRI diffusion in Iran used Ministry of Health equipment information and questionnaires to medical universities. It traced the acquisition of the first MRI unit to 1990 and described faster adoption later in the decade, with a growing private-sector contribution. The provincial distribution did not simply follow population size. The authors therefore emphasized health technology assessment: evaluating the value and appropriate use of equipment when deciding where it should be introduced. This is historical evidence about diffusion, rather than a current inventory of centers. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geographic-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More scanners can coexist with unequal access",
        "content": "Research in Lorestan, a province in western Iran, compared public-hospital CT and MRI distribution in 2014 and 2023. Equipment expanded, but the authors found greater inequality in its distribution across the study locations. Their discussion connects equipment allocation with the concentration of hospitals, specialists, and expected demand in larger cities. [s4]\n\nThe study has important boundaries. It did not establish how additional equipment changed clinical outcomes, and it cannot represent every province or private imaging center. Scanner distribution also cannot establish waiting times, affordability, machine uptime, or the availability of expert interpretation. The practical conclusion is that an imaging network should be understood through both technical capacity and access to a functioning clinical service. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
