    "description": "Home care brings professional health services into the household, including support after hospital discharge and care for people with continuing needs. Iranian qualitative research highlights the importance of family relationships, trained personnel, financing, and oversight in making these services dependable.",
    "tags": ["Home care", "Nursing", "Family caregivers", "Continuity of care", "Older people"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:06:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:06:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Home Care in Iran: Nursing, Families and Service Quality | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore professional home care in Iran through research on nursing practice, family expectations, service costs, training, and quality oversight."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "care-in-households",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Care enters a different working environment",
        "content": "Home care moves professional services into the place where a person lives. Iranian nursing research describes work extending from support after hospital discharge to care for older people and people with disabilities or continuing health needs. The household becomes a care setting, bringing clinical work into close contact with family routines and relationships. [s1]\n\nThis setting changes the conditions of practice. Nurses interviewed in Tehran and Khorasan described difficult care situations, economic pressures, professional barriers, social difficulties, and bureaucratic tensions. Their accounts show why a home visit involves more than transporting a hospital task to another address: equipment, professional support, interpersonal boundaries, and the safety of the visiting worker all shape delivery. The findings document participants’ experiences; they do not measure how often each problem occurs across Iran. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "families-and-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Families and the health system both shape provision",
        "content": "A qualitative study conducted in Iran in 2015 examined home care through interviews with policymakers, clinicians, patients, and family members. It identified a treatment-oriented health system, cultural expectations, and inadequate infrastructure as important barriers. Including patients and relatives matters because professional service design and household expectations may differ. [s2]\n\nThe study’s findings also help explain why home care cannot be understood solely as a private arrangement between a nurse and a family. Its place within the wider health system affects how services are organized and supported. The researchers argued for clearer integration, attention to cultural conditions, and suitable infrastructure. These are findings and recommendations from that research period, rather than a description of current legal entitlements or insurance coverage. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "quality-and-regional-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Quality depends on the service around the visit",
        "content": "Research conducted in Isfahan in 2022 and published in 2024 distinguished infrastructure problems from problems in the delivery process. Participants discussed public acceptance, service economics, staffing, policymaking, quality improvement, and the facilities needed to provide care. The authors recommended stronger workforce preparation, monitoring, accreditation, and attention to insurance financing. These recommendations should not be read as proof that a particular funding or accreditation arrangement has since been implemented. [s3]\n\nThe Isfahan study explicitly cautions against generalizing qualitative findings to other communities. Together, these studies provide a useful picture of recurring concerns while leaving room for differences between cities, agencies, and households. They do not establish national patient outcomes or demonstrate that home care consistently reduces expenditure. The central issue is continuity: a visit becomes more valuable when competent staff, appropriate resources, and dependable organizational support sustain the care around it. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
