    "description": "This branch explores the digital systems used to record, coordinate, and deliver healthcare in Iran. Research on electronic records and telemedicine shows both their practical potential and the organizational, infrastructure, and governance conditions needed for effective use.",
    "tags": ["Digital health", "Electronic health records", "Hospital information systems", "Telemedicine", "Health data"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:06:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:06:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Health Technology in Iran: Records, Systems and Telemedicine | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iran’s digital health systems, including SEPAS, SIB, hospital information exchange, and barriers to telemedicine."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From recording an encounter to supporting care",
        "content": "Within this branch, health technology means the digital systems that support healthcare information and communication. A useful starting distinction is between recording an encounter, exchanging information between organizations, and making that information useful during care. Iranian research shows that these are related achievements, each requiring more than the installation of software. [s1][s2]\n\nA hospital information system manages information generated within a hospital. An electronic health record has a broader ambition: maintaining information across encounters and potentially across organizations. SEPAS, Iran’s national electronic health record initiative, receives information from hospital systems through infrastructure hosted at medical universities. The transferred material described in research includes demographic details, diagnoses, procedures, test results, and billing information. This architecture establishes a route for information exchange; it does not establish that every clinician can use a complete longitudinal record at every consultation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "primary-care-records",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Primary care has its own information needs",
        "content": "SIB, Iran’s Integrated Electronic Health System, supports primary-care documentation. Its functions described in research include registering people and services, recording vaccination and pregnancy care, identifying risk factors, and supporting screening and reporting. These tasks concern continuing care for a covered population, giving the system a different working context from a hospital admission record. [s2]\n\nA qualitative study in health centers in Ahvaz, Shoushtar, and Omidiyeh, in Khuzestan province, found both benefits and difficulties. Participants described faster information retrieval, less paperwork, and improved reporting, alongside training problems, internet interruptions, costs, and additional documentation workload. Concerns also included confidentiality and whether recorded services accurately represented care delivered. These findings explain why data quality and staff experience belong in any assessment of digital health. They are local accounts, rather than national estimates of satisfaction or service improvement. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-sepas",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "National infrastructure needs evaluation in practice",
        "content": "A study of SEPAS users conducted in 2021 and published in 2023 examined system success through a structured information-systems evaluation model. Its authors identified stability problems and limited benefits for patient-centered service delivery. The research focused on personnel working with information transfer and management in selected teaching hospitals. Their experience is valuable evidence about implementation, while leaving patient experience and frontline clinical use only partly addressed. A national record repository therefore needs to be evaluated through reliability, usability, and demonstrated care benefits as well as the information it stores. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "remote-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Remote services depend on institutions as well as connections",
        "content": "Telemedicine uses communication technology to support care across distance. Iranian policymakers interviewed for research conducted in 2022 described obstacles involving policy, operating arrangements, telecommunications, public acceptance, electronic infrastructure, bureaucracy, law, and finance. They proposed stronger national commitment, a roadmap, better infrastructure, and preparation for adoption. Those proposals establish the researchers’ recommendations, rather than proof of completed reform. [s3]\n\nThe distinction between an available technology and a dependable service is especially important here. A consultation platform needs workable arrangements for professional responsibility, payment, privacy, and organizational cooperation. The study’s findings support examining these conditions together, rather than treating connectivity as the sole explanation for adoption. They do not establish the current legal position of every remote-care service or its coverage by insurance. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "information-exchange",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Hospital information exchange described in SEPAS research",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Hospital encounter\"] -->|\"Generates records\"| B[\"Hospital information system\"]\n  B -->|\"Transfers encounter information\"| C[\"Medical university SEPAS node\"]\n  C -->|\"Participates in exchange\"| D[\"National SEPAS infrastructure\"]\n  D -->|\"Stores submitted information\"| E[\"Health information repository\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This simplified diagram follows the information-transfer architecture described in the SEPAS evaluation. Its arrows represent record generation, transfer, and storage; they do not establish universal clinical access or complete coverage.",
        "as_of_date": "2021-12-31",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-branch",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following the branches",
        "content": "The child pages separate records, hospital management, telemedicine, appointment booking, analytics, and monitoring so that their different purposes can be examined directly. The strongest inspected evidence here concerns electronic records and barriers to remote care. A fuller account still needs verified evidence about booking services, analytics deployments, and monitoring tools. Across the researched systems, the useful question is how information becomes reliable and usable in care, with benefits assessed in the setting where the technology operates. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
